    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Beowulf survives only in the Nowell Codex, copied between about 975 and 1025; when the poem was composed, and whether it was passed down orally first, is still debated.</li><li>It tells of a Geatish hero who frees the Danish king Hrothgar's hall from Grendel and his mother and dies, fifty years later, fighting a dragon.</li><li>Damaged in the Ashburnham House fire of 1731, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> was first transcribed in 1786, and the poem has since been translated hundreds of times.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>'s shelfmark, Cotton Vitellius A.XV, means the 15th book from the left on the top shelf of the bookcase in Robert Cotton's library that had a bust of the Roman emperor Vitellius on top.</p></aside>
    </section>

